---
import ShareButton from '../../../components/ShareButton.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../../components/SiteFooter.astro';

const title = 'How to Change Engine Oil: Beginner’s Guide | WorkshopGirl';
const description = 'Learn how to change engine oil step by step, from preparing your tools and draining old oil to replacing the filter and adding fresh oil safely.';
const canonical = 'https://workshopgirl.com/tutorials/how-to-change-engine-oil/';
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="description" content={description} />
    <link rel="canonical" href={canonical} />
    <meta property="og:title" content={title} />
    <meta property="og:description" content={description} />
    <meta property="og:type" content="article" />
    <meta property="og:url" content={canonical} />
    <meta property="og:image" content="https://workshopgirl.com/images/tutorials/engine-oil/workshopgirl-engine-oil-preparation.webp?v=2" />
    <meta property="article:section" content="Engines" />
    <meta property="article:published_time" content="2026-09-21" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <title>{title}</title>
    <style>
      @import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;600;700&family=Space+Grotesk:wght@500;600;700&display=swap');
      :root { --ink:#171717; --muted:#655f59; --paper:#f7f4ef; --pink:#e53b7d; --orange:#e58445; --line:#ded8d0; --soft:#fffaf4; }
      * { box-sizing:border-box; }
      html { background:var(--paper); color:var(--ink); font-family:'DM Sans',sans-serif; }
      body { margin:0; min-width:320px; }
      a { color:inherit; }
      .shell { min-height:100vh; overflow:hidden; position:relative; }
      .shell::before { content:''; position:absolute; inset:0; pointer-events:none; opacity:.22; background-image:radial-gradient(#b8afa4 .7px,transparent .7px); background-size:18px 18px; mask-image:linear-gradient(to bottom,black,transparent 70%); }
      header { align-items:center; display:flex; justify-content:space-between; margin:0 auto; max-width:1180px; padding:30px 5vw; position:relative; z-index:1; }
      .brand { font-family:'Space Grotesk',sans-serif; font-size:1.2rem; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:-.04em; text-decoration:none; }
      .brand span, .eyebrow, .category { color:var(--pink); }
      nav { display:flex; gap:24px; }
      nav a { color:var(--muted); font-size:.9rem; text-decoration:none; }
      nav a:hover, nav a:focus-visible { color:var(--pink); }
      main { margin:0 auto; max-width:920px; padding:9vh 5vw 12vh; position:relative; z-index:1; }
      .back { color:var(--muted); font-size:.9rem; text-decoration:none; }
      .back:hover { color:var(--pink); }
      .eyebrow { font-size:.78rem; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.14em; margin-top:54px; text-transform:uppercase; }
      h1, h2 { font-family:'Space Grotesk',sans-serif; letter-spacing:-.065em; }
      h1 { font-size:clamp(3rem,7vw,6.2rem); line-height:.94; margin:20px 0 26px; max-width:820px; }
      .dek { color:var(--muted); font-size:1.2rem; line-height:1.7; max-width:700px; }
      .byline { border-bottom:1px solid var(--line); color:var(--muted); font-size:.86rem; margin:30px 0 54px; padding-bottom:22px; }
      .hero { margin:0 0 54px; }
      figure { margin:0; }
      figure img { display:block; height:auto; width:100%; }
      figcaption { color:var(--muted); font-size:.86rem; line-height:1.5; margin-top:12px; }
      article { font-size:1.06rem; line-height:1.78; }
      article p { margin:0 0 1.35em; }
      article h2 { font-size:clamp(2rem,4vw,3.1rem); line-height:1; margin:2.4em 0 .65em; }
      article h3 { font-family:'Space Grotesk',sans-serif; font-size:1.35rem; margin:2em 0 .5em; }
      article ul, article ol { margin:0 0 1.5em; padding-left:1.35em; }
      article li { margin:.35em 0; padding-left:.2em; }
      .safety, .tip { background:var(--soft); border-left:4px solid var(--pink); margin:28px 0 34px; padding:22px 24px; }
      .safety strong, .tip strong { display:block; font-family:'Space Grotesk',sans-serif; margin-bottom:6px; }
      .safety p, .tip p { margin:0; }
      .checklist { background:var(--soft); border:1px solid var(--line); list-style:none; padding:22px 26px; }
      .checklist li::before { color:var(--pink); content:'□'; font-size:1.2em; margin-right:10px; }
      .related { border-top:1px solid var(--line); margin-top:54px; padding-top:24px; }
      .related a { color:var(--pink); font-weight:700; }
      .closing { border-top:1px solid var(--line); margin-top:56px; padding-top:26px; }
      :focus-visible { outline:3px solid var(--pink); outline-offset:4px; }
      @media (max-width:700px) { header { padding:22px 7vw; } nav { gap:14px; } main { padding:7vh 7vw 10vh; } .eyebrow { margin-top:40px; } h1 { font-size:clamp(2.8rem,14vw,5rem); } article { font-size:1rem; } }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="shell">
      <SiteHeader current="tutorials" />
      <main>
        <a class="back" href="/tutorials/">← Back to tutorials</a>
        <div class="eyebrow">Tutorials / Engines</div>
        <h1>How to Change Engine Oil — A Beginner’s Guide</h1>
        <p class="dek">Changing engine oil is one of the most useful maintenance skills a beginner can learn. Done carefully, it gives you a better understanding of your vehicle and a solid workshop habit.</p>
        <div class="byline">Published September 21, 2026 · Workshop Girl</div>
        <ShareButton title={title} canonical={canonical} />

        <article>
          <p>The exact oil specification, capacity, drain-plug torque, filter type, and service interval vary by vehicle. Before starting, verify every vehicle-specific detail in the owner’s manual or the manufacturer’s service information.</p>

          <h2>Before You Start</h2>
          <p>Work on a stable, level surface and allow the engine to cool enough to work safely. Confirm the correct oil specification and quantity, the correct replacement filter, and that all of your tools are ready before you begin.</p>
          <div class="safety"><strong>Safety first</strong><p>Never work underneath a vehicle supported only by a hydraulic jack. If lifting is required, use properly rated jack stands at the manufacturer’s lifting points, with wheel chocks where appropriate.</p></div>
          <figure class="hero">
            <img src="/images/tutorials/engine-oil/workshopgirl-engine-oil-preparation.webp?v=2" alt="Workshop Girl preparing tools and supplies for an engine oil change beside a car." width="1672" height="941" fetchpriority="high" />
            <figcaption>Workshop Girl gets everything ready before starting the oil change.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <h2>Tools &amp; Materials</h2>
          <p>Exact requirements depend on the vehicle, but a typical oil change may need:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Correct engine oil</li><li>Replacement oil filter</li><li>Oil drain pan</li><li>Socket, ratchet, or correct drain-plug tool</li><li>Oil-filter wrench, if required</li><li>Funnel</li><li>Gloves and shop towels or rags</li><li>Jack and properly rated jack stands, if required</li><li>Wheel chocks where appropriate</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>Step 1 — Prepare the Vehicle</h2>
          <p>Park safely, engage the parking brake, and follow the manufacturer’s lifting points if the vehicle needs to be raised. Secure it properly before going underneath. Open the hood and locate the oil filler cap, drain plug, and oil filter.</p>

          <h2>Step 2 — Drain the Old Engine Oil</h2>
          <figure class="hero">
            <img src="/images/tutorials/engine-oil/workshopgirl-draining-engine-oil.webp?v=2" alt="Workshop Girl draining used engine oil from a raised car into an oil drain pan." width="1672" height="941" loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>Workshop Girl drains the used engine oil into a proper collection pan.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <p>Position the drain pan so it catches the oil, remembering that the flow direction can change as draining slows. Carefully loosen the drain plug and allow the oil to drain fully. Inspect the plug and sealing washer or gasket, replacing the washer or gasket when the manufacturer specifies it.</p>
          <div class="safety"><strong>Never rush this part</strong><p>Never crawl underneath a vehicle supported only by a hydraulic jack. Confirm the vehicle is securely supported before reaching under it.</p></div>

          <h2>Step 3 — Replace the Oil Filter</h2>
          <p>Place the drain pan underneath the filter because residual oil may spill. Remove the old filter and check that its gasket did not remain attached to the engine. Where applicable, lightly lubricate the new filter gasket with clean engine oil, then install the filter according to its instructions. Do not invent a tightening specification; follow the filter or vehicle manufacturer’s guidance.</p>

          <h2>Step 4 — Reinstall the Drain Plug</h2>
          <p>Clean the area around the drain opening. Install the correct washer or seal if required, reinstall the drain plug, and tighten it to the manufacturer’s specification. Do not guess the torque value.</p>

          <h2>Step 5 — Add Fresh Engine Oil</h2>
          <figure class="hero">
            <img src="/images/tutorials/engine-oil/workshopgirl-adding-fresh-engine-oil.webp?v=2" alt="Workshop Girl pouring fresh engine oil through a funnel into the engine." width="1672" height="941" loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>Workshop Girl adds fresh engine oil through a funnel.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <p>Verify that the drain plug and filter are installed. If the vehicle was lifted, lower it safely before the final level check. Add the manufacturer-specified oil gradually through a funnel, taking care not to overfill. Reinstall the oil filler cap.</p>

          <h2>Step 6 — Start the Engine and Check</h2>
          <p>Start the engine and verify that the oil-pressure warning indicator behaves normally. Let the engine run briefly, then switch it off. Inspect the drain plug and filter area for leaks. Wait the period recommended by the manufacturer before checking the level. Use the dipstick if the vehicle has one; some modern vehicles use an electronic oil-level measurement instead. Add only small amounts if necessary.</p>

          <h2>Step 7 — Clean Up and Dispose of Used Oil</h2>
          <p>Transfer the used oil into a sealed, suitable container. Never pour it onto the ground, into drains, or into household waste. Take it to an appropriate used-oil collection or recycling facility according to local regulations, and recycle or dispose of the used filter appropriately.</p>

          <h2>Common Beginner Mistakes</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Using the wrong oil specification</li><li>Overfilling</li><li>Forgetting a drain-plug washer</li><li>Double-gasketing the oil filter</li><li>Overtightening the filter</li><li>Guessing drain-plug torque</li><li>Forgetting to reinstall the oil filler cap</li><li>Failing to check for leaks</li><li>Working underneath an improperly supported vehicle</li>
          </ul>

          <div class="tip"><strong>Workshop Girl tip</strong><p>Take your time. A clean, organized workspace makes even a simple oil change safer and easier.</p></div>

          <h2>Quick Checklist Before You’re Done</h2>
          <ul class="checklist">
            <li>Drain plug installed correctly</li><li>Oil filter installed correctly</li><li>Correct amount and specification of oil added</li><li>Oil filler cap installed</li><li>Oil level checked</li><li>No visible leaks</li><li>Tools removed from the engine bay</li><li>Used oil stored for proper recycling</li>
          </ul>

          <div class="related"><strong>Keep learning:</strong> <a href="/tutorials/how-to-change-a-flat-tire/">How to Change a Flat Tire — A Beginner’s Guide</a> · <a href="/tutorials/how-to-replace-brake-pads/">How to Replace Brake Pads — A Beginner’s Guide</a> · <a href="/tutorials/engine-air-filter/">How to Inspect, Clean or Replace Your Engine Air Filter</a></div>
          <div class="closing"><h2>Final Thoughts</h2><p>An oil change is a practical way to start understanding your machine. The procedure is straightforward, but the details belong to the specific vehicle in front of you. Check the manual, work methodically, and let good workshop habits do the heavy lifting.</p></div>
        </article>
      </main><SiteFooter />
    </div>
  </body>
</html>
